# Line endings are normalized to LF in the repository and in every working
# tree, regardless of each developer's core.autocrlf. Without this, a Windows
# checkout rewrites all 106 files to CRLF and the next Linux or CI checkout
# sees every line of every file as modified.
#
# eol=lf rather than bare text=auto: text=auto alone normalizes what is stored
# but leaves the working copy to core.autocrlf, so the churn returns per
# machine. Pinning both ends is what makes the tree identical everywhere.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Deliberate NUL bytes — canonical-JSON fuzz fixtures exercising RFC 8785
# against control characters, alongside the unicode and trailing-space cases.
# Git already classifies this as binary under text=auto and leaves it alone;
# declaring it keeps that true if anyone later adds an explicit `*.ts text`
# rule, which would rewrite those bytes and silently weaken the test.
test/portal/verify-client.test.ts -text

# Endings these formats actually require, irrespective of the rule above.
*.bat text eol=crlf
*.cmd text eol=crlf
*.sh  text eol=lf

# Binary formats, should any arrive. Prevents diff noise and eol mangling.
*.png  binary
*.jpg  binary
*.jpeg binary
*.gif  binary
*.ico  binary
*.pdf  binary
*.woff binary
*.woff2 binary
